Z||000003||Dale||09-12-1999||03:41 AM||||OK,  in most cases 5 speeds are better becouse the gears ratios are a little better matched (closer) also the 5 speeds if I am not mistaken are overdrive.  My 4 speed tops out at about 85mph, not that I hit that speed much but when passing I run out of engine.  I am surprised how close the gears are on the 4spd for a stock trany.  I cant find the gear ratios at the moment.  You might post a question on the tech forum for that.  I also think that 1st gear is to low in the 4 spd, and probably the 5spd too.||||reg||7
Z||000004||Dale||09-12-1999||03:47 AM||||Boy, your up late.<BR>Aloha||||reg||1
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||04:00 AM||||I had an 86 SE 4spd with the 2.8 V6. That motor is now mated to an Isuzu 5spd in my 88. In reality you would think the 4spd would be faster off the line, but the Isuzu has the lowest 1st gear (3.73) compare to the 4spd 1st gear(3.31)and the Getag (3.50). I myself would never go back to the notchy 4spd. This 5spd cruises nicely in 5th gear at 80 mph and I gained some milage too. However, the 4spd took many years of abuse and is still just fine, other than being a little notchy. My Isuzu is doing fine with my heavy foot and quite a few mean second gear chirps. If it ever does fail I do have an extra Getrag and my old 4spd, but as long as it holds up(I think it will have no problems) it stays in.||||reg||3
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||04:04 AM||||4spd gear ratios:<BR>1st - 3.31<BR>2nd - 1.95<BR>3rd - 1.24<BR>4th - 0.81<P>Isuzu 5spd:<BR>1st - 3.73<BR>2nd - 2.04<BR>3rd - 1.45<BR>4th - 1.03<BR>5th - 0.74<P>Getrag 5spd:<BR>1st - 3.50<BR>2nd - 2.05<BR>3rd - 1.38<BR>4th - 0.94<BR>5th - 0.72||||reg||7
Z||000007||Dale||09-12-1999||04:15 AM||||So you do get a little bit more out of the 5 spd. SCCA does it have the same type of sloppy linkage as the 4 spd.  I also didn't realize the 4spd had that much of an OD Hmmm!<BR>your up late too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It's 10pm here and can hardly stay awake anymore (lightweight)||||reg||1
Z||000008||fastonpumpgas||09-12-1999||04:24 AM||||ok, since it looks like 5 spped is the way to go, the trick is finding one huh ... I want to put this car together once .. I am trying for 200 plus ponys out of this 3.1<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||07:09 AM||||Dale, my friend put an Isuzu in his 86 GT to replace the 4spd. He had the 4spd shifter, but installed the 5spd shifter from the donar car, but his it a bit sloppy compared to mine. I did not mess with my shifter (other than adding a shortened stick) or my cables because they were in the car already. My Fiero shifts AWESOME. I don't even lift my foot from the gas when shifting from 2nd to 3rd when I am racing and have never missed. I can do it from 1st to 2nd too, but I missed once and decided to not do that anymore. I could never shift my 4spd that fast.<P>fastonpumpgas, The friend I referred to got his Isuzu tranny from a local boneyard for $150 including the shifter and cables. I think the Isuzu is pretty easy to find, but the Getrag is <B>impossible</B> to find. ||||reg||3
Z||000010||chris6878||09-12-1999||11:20 AM||||which is better the 5-speed Isuzu or the Getrag..????||||reg||1
Z||000011||88 Fiero||09-12-1999||11:28 AM||||This is a very interesting subject....which to choose 4-Speed or a 5-Speed...The question is that how are you planning to drive your car...are you going to race it??? If so I would go with a 4-Speed...A 5-Speed is good on a long haul..but if you are planning to race 1/4 mile...your better off with a 4-Speed...I know the gear ratios of the different tranny of the Fiero's ..starting from 3.31 all the way to 3.73 (Izusu)...but has anyone tried hooking up a 4.10 gear on a Fiero 4-Speed???? I guess not...My freind Keith at Fiero Motorsports has one and believe me that damn car is quick in acceleration....I've race him and I got a 5-Speed...same engine at that time...the only thing about the 4.10 gears is that it's loud..but using it as an advantage is very, very good..and it can be done on a 4-Speed tranny... The IMSA Fiero's and the real Fiero Pace Car is equipped with the 4.10 gear and that's how Keith got the idea and now he loves it!!!! Sorry guys...been with this Fiero Crap thing too long...since early 1985 when I bought my first fiero 4 cylinder..ever since I've been hook with this car and it's racing histories...so pretty much knows something about this car and it's ailments...been to the Fiero Plant once...also been a long time member of FOCOA....<P>[This message has been edited by 88 Fiero (edited 09-12-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by 88 Fiero (edited 09-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||88 Fiero||09-12-1999||11:36 AM||||hmmm..forgot one thing to say....the Izusu 5-Speed are great...but if you are planning to go all out horse power...this is not the tranny you want to use...I wil guarantee it will break and it's been proven...the reason for the gear ratios of 3:73 is because the later model 4 cylinders are really under power...with the use of this gear it helps to compensate the difference.... I've seen Fiero's with this tranny and the owners doesn't even want to use the car for racing...the critical part of that tranny is getting off in first gear....once moving you could power shift to different gears...If you try to accelerate hard with this tranny with so much horsepower...your going to need a basket underneath the car....but other than that...it's a very good tranny....The Getrag Tranny on the other hand is a choice for everyone using a Turbo or a V8 kit in their car...The Getrag can handle pretty much of the torque as well as horsepower...One thing that I know that a 5-Speed Getrag will suffer is when you pass the 275-300 horsepower...that's where the clucth will fall apart...Charlie who own's a 1988 Fiero GT and have Keith of Fiero Motorsports hook up one of Gary Zumalt V8 kit and now he got a LT1 corvette engine..he got headers,computer chip...the car is awsome...but he is having problems with his clutch...it's always dissentigrating all the time...He gas gone to alot of different clutch brand...I think he had one of the clutch manufacturers to make him a special clutch...more of a racing type...so far it's holding...Charlie's background is.. he race cars in Europe, specializing in Porsches..so the guy knows what he is looking for and was very much impress with the LT1 engine in a Fiero...<p>[This message has been edited by 88 Fiero (edited 09-12-1999).]||||reg||7
Z||000013||88 Fiero||09-12-1999||11:52 AM||||ooopppsss...here I go again....SCCA you forgot that the model yr of 1986-1987 Fiero with the 5-Speed V6 tranny has a different gear ratios than the 5-Speed V6 '88's... Here is the specs:<P>Getrag 5-Speed<P>1st - 3.92<BR>2nd - 2.19<BR>3rd - 1.38<BR>4th - 0.94<BR>5th - 0.72<BR>Rev - 3.41<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88 Fiero (edited 09-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||09-12-1999||12:49 PM||||Lots of good info in this discussion. Here's my opinion. I have driven Fieros with all four types of trannies (auto, 4 speed, 5 speed Isuzu, and 5 speed Getrag/Muncie). The auto is good, holds up well, and is easy to drive. Both 5 speeds shift smoothly and make the best sport/street tranny with the extra gearing and cruises easy with the overdrive. The Getrag is more desireable because it's heavier duty. You can get the Getrag out of some Berettas and other front wheel drive cars and then buy a kit to adapt it to the Fiero. My latest Fiero is a 85 GT with a 4 speed. First and reverse gears are a little hard to find at first, but since I've got used to driving it, I have no trouble slamming through the gears. The 4 speed is a tough tranny and is quicker at the dragstrip (one less shift). I admit the 5 speed linkage/cables seem to give it a slightly smoother shift, but I have yet to notice any real "notchy" problems with my 4 speed setup. I'm not switching it unless it goes completely out, I like my 4 speed.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Greg Piet||09-12-1999||04:53 PM||||Kick hill advertises on his web site that he has the muncie conversion kits with used trannys, for only about 400 bucks.  I wish I knew about that before my 4 speed went out on me.||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||09-12-1999||08:22 PM||||Rodney Dickman has FWD Getrag conversion kits for the Fiero, plus other neat stuff too.<P> <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/</A> ||||reg||3
Z||000017||fastonpumpgas||09-12-1999||09:42 PM||||well I guess I should have said that I plan to get a super charger in due time,I'm putting forged piston maybe a small nitros kit I am not sure, but It is definately for 1/4 mile, I dont care if it will do 125, but I want to get to 100 in 14  !!!!!  WOO HOO  .. I am planning on keeping the 4speed ,and go to a heavy duty clutch .. i dont think I can top 300 horses, but 250 I think I can do, What is everyones opinion on the strenth of the differant trannies, I dont want to get into kits or converstion, I want to have a fiero !!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||09-13-1999||12:34 AM||||For high performance, the only clutch I'd use would be a Centerforce, and they do make them for a Fiero I've been told. Your 4 speed tranny should hold up ok, if it's still in good shape. But if you're really planning on abusing it regularly at the strip, you might consider having it rebuilt to be safe.||||reg||1
Z||000019||ChadMan||09-13-1999||08:55 AM||||My 84 4-speed (4.10) was the absolute king of the stoplight grand prix - until I shifted to second gear, that is.  The only way I would mate this tranny to anything more than the Iron Duke is to drag race.  The shifting action was terrible, too.<P>My 86 4-speed V6 shifts better, but not by much.  It is quick off the line, though.  I've driven a couple of Getrag-equipped Fieros and mine feels quicker, though I'd gladly pay the price for the slicker shifts and nice quiet 5th gear.<P>The Isuzu in my 88 Coupe is my favorite.  It shifts as smoothly now at 102K as it did when I bought the car at 68K.  People say it's not durable, but I've seen 5 or 6 Fieros now with V6's or V8's matched up to the Isuzu and never heard of any problems (V8 Archie backs this up).<P>To quote Forrest Gump, "That's all I've got to say about that."||||reg||1
Z||000020||Dale||09-13-1999||01:45 PM||||Life is like garage full of fiero's, you never really know what your gonna get.||||reg||1
Z||000021||fastonpumpgas||09-13-1999||10:43 PM||||yeah I opened my 4 speed up today and it loks great, now as long as I dont run over my Crankshaft I think I will be alright, I should have it all done but october, then I will go looking for some 5.0's   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
